naturally monumental expectations  accompany the first halflife game in 13  years and for the iconic franchises  return to come in the form of a VR  exclusive is undoubtedly bold but at  each step of the way  half-life Alex proves that almost  everything the franchise did best  thrives in VR the environmental puzzles  that require a keen eye  the threat of a headcrab jumping for  your face the cryptic storytelling the  series staples are as powerful as ever  here and in its greatest moments  half-life Alex confidently shows you why  it couldn't have been done any other way  a true half-life form the entire game  goes from morning to night in a single  shot of first-person action in which you  as Alyx Vance trek through city 17  at first it's the savior dad Eli Vance  from the clutches of the combine which  then leads to uncovering the nature of  the vault that massive floating  structure in the sky with a cheeky  sidekick Russell in your ear and a  trusty prophetic vordigan who comes in  clutch Alex is more than prepared  there's a newfound intimacy captured in  doing the things at half life always  asks of you because it's a vr game the  way you look at and process your  surroundings fundamentally changes thus  making the solutions to environmental  puzzles more of a personal  accomplishment than before simply  finding the right objects to progress  was fine with a keyboard and mouse but  when it's your own hands turning the  valves pulling the levers or hitting the  switches while turning your head to see  the results  these become enticing gameplay mechanics  rather than a means of breaking up the  pace without waypoints or objective  markers to guide you subtle visual cues  and calculate a level design lead you to  the solutions and progress feels earned  because of that you may not have the  gravity gun here but the spirit of its  physics based interaction lives through  the gravity gloves both as a sensible  thematic fit and tool for proper VR  gameplay they allow you to magnetically  pull objects from afar and catching them  midair is always satisfying especially  when snatching a grenade off a combine  soldier to throw it back in their face  which just as important is Alex's  multi-tool which serves as a way to  engage in the game simple yet enjoyable  spatial puzzles rewiring circuitry to  unlock paths forward is the multi-tools  most crucial function though so you'll  need a sharp eye tracing electrical  currents and cables to progress  half-life Alex revolves around the  balance of puzzle elements and  suspenseful combat it might not have the  bombastic fights from series pass but  most of that has been exchanged for  close encounters sometimes tapping into  horror that half-life had only  previously toyed with headcrabs aren't  just annoying pests they will literally  latch on to your head or cause the  occasional jump scare the same goes for  barnacles you do not want your own  virtual body dragged up toward the  ceiling by its disgusting slimy tongue  other scenarios have you navigating  through pitch-black darkness with your  wrist mounted flashlights there's also  an entire chapter dedicated to an  invincible mutant that can't see but has  sharp hearing to chase you down combine  soldiers may still be dummies but when  they're coming for you and VR their  threat becomes imminent  you'll physically duck in and out of  cover and peek around corners ready to  bust shots and frantically reload like  any good VR shooter requires though here  in its distinctly half-life form  you'll hear a combine radio chatter and  the high-pitched ring of them flatlining  all the while the odd comfort of the  old-school techno beats during the  heated firefights kick in there are many  types of combine soldiers or styles of  encounters but I was always eager to  face them head-on in each scenario alex  packs light when it comes to weapons  with only a pistol shotgun and SMG all  three have a few upgrades to make them  more effective which must be done at  combine fabricator stations using  resident the only real collectible item  in the game animals often scarce and  resins tucked away around corners so  scavenging is a core element further  emphasizing Alex's scrappy nature and  honestly the slim arsenal fits the  styled combat sequences with each weapon  effective in their own right  overall half-life Alex takes many of the  concepts we've seen evolve since V ARS  inception and distills them to their  fundamentals it brings them together to  create a VR experience that's a full  cohesive whole and the game gives  multiple accessibility options to  mitigate motion sickness or make the  game playable if you have physical  limitations all that said there are a  few shortcomings doors and mechanisms  you need to grip don't always react to  your movements the way you would expect  and sometimes they're just too many  unimportant objects that obscure the  thing that you're actually trying to  pull in with their gravity gloves  thankfully these instances are rare  enough as to not drag down otherwise  intuitive mechanics now the front half  of the game does settle into a bit of a  routine you may start to see through  some of the trite aspects of the combat  challenges or scripted sequences and the  reliance on narrow corridors for  stretches and one point I wondered where  the game was going but there's a turning  point  and the practice routines pay off as the  game escalates the stakes you'll be  struck by the awe-inspiring sights the  thrill of firefights that ramp up in  intensity while performing the VR  specific mechanics and the unbearable  suspense of some levels yet all those  pale in comparison to the final hour  when half-life Alex solidifies itself as  the boldest the series has ever been the  very concept of VR becomes the core  narrative device your hands and by  extension  Alex's actions are fundamental to the  delivery of its monumental moment only  then will you really understand why VR  was the only way this game could have  even existed and it delivers something  that's surreal revelatory and incredibly  empowering half-life alex has  far-reaching implications for the future  of the franchise both and where it goes  next and what forms future games can  even take and in true half-life fashion  more questions and answers linger but  for good reason  and now without a reminder of why you  love the series to begin with it made  good on its shift to VR elevating many  of the aspects we've come to love about  the series the intimacy of VR brings you  closer to a world you thought you knew  over the past 22 years and as it  concludes half-life Alex will hit you  with powerful unforgettable moments that  truly transcend VR tropes and make the  13-year weight all worth it  [Music]
 

